  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The keystore is the only secret store",
      "items": [
        "tokens live behind SecretStorePort — Windows Credential Manager, macOS Keychain, libsecret — and nowhere else",
        "SQLite, config, logs, crash dumps and the binary hold no token: a secret in SQLite or a log is a breach on a shared machine",
        "sign-out and tenant switch clear the keystore and the SQLite rows in the same operation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "OIDC + PKCE in the system browser",
      "items": [
        "the app never sees the password: it opens the system browser with a PKCE verifier and a state value, and receives a loopback or custom-scheme redirect",
        "the authorization code plus the verifier go to the token endpoint; the exchange happens off the GUI thread, never as a blocking call",
        "the refresh token rotates with reuse detection: a replayed token revokes the whole family and the ViewModel lands on a Forbidden/Error status"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Policy beats the user",
      "items": [
        "managed policy wins over user settings, and a kill switch applies immediately — the ViewModel surfaces PolicyBlocked, one of the eight status values",
        "authorization is server-side: the UI hides actions, it never decides access",
        "TLS is always on with a custom CA bundle where an enterprise requires it; there is no --insecure default"
      ]
    }
  ]
